FinTracker Frontend — Critical Code Review

  ---
  1. Code Understanding

  Architecture: React 18 + Redux Toolkit + React Router v7 + Material UI + Tailwind CSS v4. It's a SPA with lazy-loaded auth pages,  
  RTK Query for data fetching, and two distinct UI systems running side by side (MUI for auth, Tailwind for the dashboard).

  State management: Three slices (AuthSlice, PersonalExpenseSlice, CategorySlice) plus RTK Query for server state. The separation is 
  reasonable, but CategorySlice exists while categories are fetched via RTK Query — the slice is dead weight.

  Data flow: Clean one-way flow: thunks/mutations → Redux → components via selectors. No context, no prop-drilling mess. Good.       

  Assumptions baked into the code:
  - Users always have localStorage access (OTP timer, email storage)
  - Currency is always CAD (hardcoded in formatCurrency and currency = "CAD")
  - A single iconRef is shared across every row in the expense table — this is a bug

  ---
  2. Code Quality Review

  Critical bugs

  iconRef is shared across all table rows (ExpenseList.tsx:40, 226)

  Every <MoreHorizIcon ref={iconRef} /> in the table points at the same ref. The click-outside detection reads iconRef.current to    
  decide if the user clicked on "the icon" — but it only ever holds a reference to the last rendered row's icon, making
  close-on-outside-click unreliable for every row except the last one.

  // Current — one ref shared across N rows
  const iconRef = useRef<SVGSVGElement | null>(null);

  // Fix: use a data attribute instead of a ref for the icon
  const toggleMenu = useCallback((e: React.MouseEvent, expenseId: string) => {
    e.stopPropagation();
    setSelectedExpenseId(prev => prev === expenseId ? null : expenseId);
  }, []);

  // In the click-outside handler, check data attribute:
  const clickedOnIcon = (e.target as Element).closest('[data-menu-icon]');

  handleSubmit calls event.preventDefault() at the wrong place (RegisterUserPage.tsx:82-125)

  The form guard event.preventDefault() is called at the bottom of the function on line 124, after new FormData(event.currentTarget) 
  has already been called. If validation fails (nameError || emailError || passwordError), line 84 calls event.preventDefault() —    
  fine. But if passwords don't match, no preventDefault() is called before the else block, so the form can submit natively. The      
  correct pattern is to call event.preventDefault() as the very first thing.

  // BEFORE (line 82)
  const handleSubmit = (event: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    if (nameError || emailError || passwordError) {
      event.preventDefault(); // only called in one branch
      return;
    }
    // ...
    event.preventDefault(); // called at the bottom as afterthought
  };

  // AFTER
  const handleSubmit = (event: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    event.preventDefault(); // ALWAYS first
    if (nameError || emailError || passwordError) return;
    // ...
  };

  isValid state is a lie (LoginPage.tsx:29, RegisterUserPage.tsx:40)

  isValid is set to true when any single field passes validation. If the user types a valid email and then a bad password, isValid   
  stays true (set by the email validator). The button's disabled guard catches this via emailError || passwordError, but isValid     
  itself is meaningless as a "form is complete" signal. It's never tracking all-fields-valid simultaneously.

  ValidateInputs.tsx — all validators share the same setValid setter

  Every validator (ValidateEmail, ValidatePassword, ValidateName) calls setValid(true) on success and setValid(false) on failure.    
  They overwrite each other. This means the last field to be validated controls isValid for the whole form. If a user fills name     
  (valid → setValid(true)), then email (invalid → setValid(false)), then fixes email (valid → setValid(true)) — isValid is true even 
  if name was cleared afterwards. The whole isValid mechanism needs a redesign using a proper useForm pattern or at minimum tracking 
  each field independently.

  Modal setTimeout leaks (LoginPage.tsx:70-72, RegisterUserPage.tsx:76-78)

  // Current — timeout fires even after component unmounts
  setTimeout(function() {
    setModal(false);
  }, 2000);

  This runs every time error, isError, or message changes — not just on first trigger. More critically, there's no cleanup. If the   
  component unmounts before 2 seconds, you get a setState on an unmounted component (React 18 suppresses the warning but the closure 
  still holds the setter). Use useEffect with cleanup:

  useEffect(() => {
    if (!modal) return;
    const id = setTimeout(() => setModal(false), 2000);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [modal]);

  getExpiryTime() called inside useTimer initializer (OtpVerificationPage.tsx:47)

  getExpiryTime() has a side effect — it writes to localStorage. It's being called during render as part of the useTimer
  initialization object literal. On strict mode double-render this runs twice, creating two different expiry times in storage.       
  Extract it outside:

  const [expiryTime] = useState(getExpiryTime); // pass as initializer fn, not call

  Hardcoded console.warn with internal URLs in production (OtpVerificationPage.tsx:74-75)

  console.warn("Requesting OTP for email:", userEmail);
  console.warn("Resend URL:", resendUrl); // exposes backend URL

  These must be removed. There's no logger abstraction at all — just delete them.

  new File([], expense.receiptUrl) anti-pattern (AddExpense.tsx:262)

  A dummy File object is created with an empty blob just to carry a URL string in a File type field. This is a type system workaround
   that actively harms the code — file.size === 0 so the size check on line 304 (form.receipt.size > 0) will always be false for     
  existing receipts, meaning the URL never gets re-uploaded on edit. That's the right behavior but achieved through a misleading     
  hack. Use a discriminated union instead:

  type ReceiptState =
    | { kind: 'none' }
    | { kind: 'existing'; url: string }
    | { kind: 'new'; file: File; preview: string };

