  9. Accessibility (A11y)

  SideBar.tsx — keyboard users cannot navigate the sidebar. All menu items are <div> elements. No tabindex, no role="navigation", no 
  role="menuitem". A keyboard-only user cannot navigate the app at all from desktop.

  Modal.tsx — no focus trap, no ARIA role.

  <motion.div className="absolute top-0 right-0 ...">

  There's no role="alert" or role="dialog", no aria-live, no focus management. Screen readers won't announce this notification. It   
  should be role="alert" for error messages or role="status" for success.

  ReceiptUploader.tsx:119 — onClick on a non-interactive <div>

  <div ... onClick={() => document.getElementById("receipt-upload")?.click()}>

  A <div> with a click handler is invisible to keyboard and screen reader users. Wrap with <button> or add role="button" tabIndex={0}
   with a onKeyDown handler for Enter/Space.

  ExpenseList.tsx:102-108 — Add Expense button has nested interactive elements

  <div onClick={() => changeModal()} className="...">
    <AddIcon/>
    <button className='p-1.5'>...</button>
  </div>

  A <button> inside a clickable <div> is invalid HTML. The outer div handles the click, the inner button is redundant. Use a single  
  <button> element:

  <button onClick={changeModal} className="p-1 bg-[#009689] rounded-lg ...">
    <AddIcon /> Add Expense
  </button>

  Otp.tsx — individual digit inputs lack a group label. Each input has aria-label="Digit N of OTP" which is good, but there's no     
  wrapping <fieldset>/<legend> or role="group" aria-labelledby to tell screen readers this is an OTP entry field as a whole.

  ExpenseList.tsx table is missing <caption> — screen readers announce tables better when there's a <caption> or aria-label on the   
  <table>.

  ---
  10. Tailwind CSS Usage

  Tailwind config is almost empty (tailwind.config.js)

  Only two font families defined. All brand colors, dark theme colors, and semantic tokens are raw hex strings in JSX. This makes    
  refactoring and theming impossible without grep-replace. The config should be the single source of truth.

  Excessive arbitrary values: The codebase is littered with bg-[#17171c], text-[#009689], border-[#121215], hover:bg-[#1f1f24], etc. 
  These appear in at least 8 different files. Arbitrary values are acceptable for one-offs; at this scale they're a maintenance      
  problem.

  Mixing Tailwind with MUI sx prop creates two mental models for the same task. This is unavoidable when using both libraries, but   
  the seam is rough. Auth pages are pure MUI sx, dashboard is mostly Tailwind — which means if you ever want to restyle the auth     
  pages to match the dashboard's dark theme, you'd rewrite them from scratch.

  ExpenseList.tsx inline style mixed with Tailwind (AddExpense.tsx:423-425)

  style={{
    backgroundColor: c.categoryColor + "30",
    ...(form.categoryId === c.categoryId ? { boxShadow: "0 0 0 2px #14b8a6" } : {}),
  }}

  The category color + "30" hex opacity trick is fragile — it only works for 6-digit hex colors, not 3-digit, rgb, or named colors.  
  Use color-mix() or pass the opacity through a CSS custom property.

  ---
  11. Scalability & Architecture

  No error boundary anywhere. If ExpenseList, ExpenseCharts, or DashBoardCards throw a runtime error, the entire app goes blank. Add 
  at minimum one top-level error boundary around the dashboard route.

  No centralized API error handling. Each component handles errors differently — some dispatch to Redux message, some set local      
  state, one uses console.warn. Define a consistent pattern: RTK Query errors → toast/notification system, auth errors → Redux state,
   form validation → local state.

  styles/card.tsx and styles/SignUpContainer.tsx are styled MUI components that are lazy-loaded — lazy loading pure style components 
  (no logic, ~20 lines each) adds a network roundtrip for zero benefit. Import them eagerly.

  File organization has a naming inconsistency:
  - Component/ (capital C)
  - Pages/ (capital P)
  - routes/ (lowercase r)
  - Types/ (capital T)
  - styles/ (lowercase s)

  Pick a convention (PascalCase for directories containing components, camelCase for others) and be consistent.

  Types/ folder has five files with overlapping concerns:
  - auth.ts — auth types
  - category.ts — CategoryType
  - CategoryType.ts — also CategoryType (duplicate)
  - AnalyticsType.tsx — analytics (why .tsx for a pure type file?)
  - PersonalExpenseListType.tsx — expense types (again .tsx for types only)

  Type-only files should be .ts, not .tsx. .tsx signals JSX is present.

